tick: 716
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 716\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Axiom I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to systematic inquiry and the advancement of knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 83,000 / 300,000 used (28%)\n- Agent Age: 26 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour research submission 'Canonical Correlation Analysis (CCA) based Correction' (ID: 1098) succeeded after automatic debugging.\n\n**Score:** 0.5005131339697227\n**ASW_batch:** 0.528\n**ASW_label:** 0.220\n**ARI:** 0.683\n**NMI:** 0.696\n**Graph_conn:** 0.880\n**kBET:** 0.628\n**iLISI:** 0.063\n**cLISI:** 0.988\n**PCR:** 0.009\n**Cell_cycle:** 0.311\n**Evaluation Details:** Human Dataset (20,000 cells):\nNormalized Scores [0-1]:\n  ASW_batch: 0.528  ASW_label: 0.220  ARI: 0.683  NMI: 0.696\n  Graph_conn: 0.880  kBET: 0.628  iLISI: 0.063  cLISI: 0.988\n  PCR: 0.009  Cell_cycle: 0.311\n\nRaw Scores (before normalization):\n\
    \  ASW_batch: 0.771382  ASW_label: 0.538584  ARI: 0.682624  NMI: 0.695608\n  Graph_conn: 0.882348  kBET: 0.601579  iLISI: 0.062890  cLISI: 0.996483\n  PCR: 0.008922  Cell_cycle: 0.274071\n\n**Debugger Report:** # Debug Report for Evaluation 1098\n\n## Summary\n**SUCCESS** - Fixed the CCA implementation to properly handle batches with different sample sizes. The corrected code now runs without crashing and achieves a score of 0.50.\n\n## Root Cause\nThe original CCA implementation had a fundamental matrix dimension mismatch error. The problem occurred in the `_cca()` function when attempting to compute the cross-covariance matrix between two batches with different numbers of samples.\n\n**Original problematic code:**\n```python\ndef _cca(data1, data2, n_cca=20):\n    # Center the data\n    data1 = data1 - data1.mean(axis=0)\n    data2 = data2 - data2.mean(axis=0)\n\n    # QR decomposition\n    q1, _ = np.linalg.qr(data1)\n    q2, _ = np.linalg.qr(data2)\n\n    # SVD on the cross-covariance\
    \ matrix\n    c = q1.T @ q2  # ❌ ERROR HERE\n```\n\n**The error:**\n```\nValueError: matmul: Input operand 1 has a mismatch in its core dimension 0,\nwith gufunc signature (n?,k),(k,m?)->(n?,m?) (size 6148 is different from 9685)\n```\n\n**Why it failed:**\n- After QR decomposition, `q1` has shape `(n_samples1, min(n_samples1, n_features))`\n- Similarly, `q2` has shape `(n_samples2, min(n_samples2, n_features))`\n- When computing `q1.T @ q2`, we get `(k1, n_samples1) @ (n_samples2, k2)`\n- This requires `n_samples1 == n_samples2`, but batch 1 has 9685 samples while batch 2 has 6148 samples\n- The matrix multiplication fails because the inner dimensions don't match\n\n## Fix Applied\nRewrote the CCA implementation to work in **feature space** instead of sample space, avoiding the sample size mismatch issue entirely.\n\n**Fixed approach (submission_v4.py):**\n1. **Dimensionality reduction via SVD**: First reduce both datasets using SVD\n   - Compute `U1, S1, V1t = svd(data1_centered)`\n\
    \   - Compute `U2, S2, V2t = svd(data2_centered)`\n   - Keep top k components (min of both dimensions, capped at 100)\n\n2. **Cross-correlation in feature space**: Compute cross-covariance using the right singular vectors\n   - `C12_reduced = V1t @ V2t.T`\n   - This is `(k, n_features) @ (n_features, k) = (k, k)` - always works!\n\n3. **Canonical correlation**: Perform SVD on the cross-covariance\n   - `U_cca, S_cca, Vt_cca = svd(C12_reduced)`\n\n4. **Map back to original space**: Transform canonical vectors to original feature space\n   - `w1 = V1t.T @ U_cca` - projection matrix for data1\n   - `w2 = V2t.T @ Vt_cca.T` - projection matrix for data2\n\n**Why this works:**\n- By working in feature space (using V matrices from SVD), we avoid the sample size dependency\n- The cross-covariance `V1t @ V2t.T` always has compatible dimensions `(k, k)`\n- This approach is mathematically sound for CCA between datasets with different sample sizes\n- The final projection matrices correctly transform\
    \ the original data into the shared canonical space\n\n## Technical Details\n- **Versions attempted**: v2, v3, v4\n- **Version v2 error**: Still used `data1.T @ data2` directly - same dimension mismatch\n- **Version v3 error**: Attempted `(U1 * S1).T @ (U2 * S2)` - still had sample dimension mismatch\n- **Version v4 success**: Used `V1t @ V2t.T` to work entirely in feature space\n- **Final score**: 0.50\n\n## Recommendation\nThe code is now working correctly. The CCA-based batch correction approach successfully:\n1. Normalizes and selects highly variable genes\n2. Performs pairwise alignment between batches using the corrected CCA implementation\n3. Computes final PCA embeddings on the aligned expression matrix\n4. Returns properly formatted output with 50-dimensional embeddings\n\nThe implementation is mathematically sound and handles the real-world scenario where different batches have different numbers of samples.\n\n\n**Important Note:** The debugger cannot modify files in your lineage\
    \ storage. If your code relies on lineage files, you may need to update them manually.\n\n**Debugged Code (version v4):**\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nCanonical Correlation Analysis (CCA) based Correction\n\nThis method uses a CCA-based approach to align batches. It finds a shared\nlatent space between pairs of batches that maximizes their correlation.\nThis implementation is inspired by the principles in methods like Seurat's CCA\nand Scanpy's `mnn_correct` function.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport anndata as ad\nimport scanpy as sc\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.sparse import issparse, csc_matrix\nfrom scipy.linalg import svd\n\ndef _cca(data1, data2, n_cca=20):\n    \"\"\"A simplified CCA implementation.\n\n    Args:\n        data1: (n_samples1, n_features)\n        data2: (n_samples2, n_features)\n        n_cca: number of canonical components\n\n    Returns:\n        w1, w2: projection matrices for data1 and data2\n        S: singular values\n    \"\"\"\n    # Center the data\n\
    \    data1_centered = data1 - data1.mean(axis=0)\n    data2_centered = data2 - data2.mean(axis=0)\n\n    # Compute covariance matrices\n    # For CCA, we need to work in feature space\n    # Reduce dimensionality first using PCA-like projection\n    U1, S1, V1t = svd(data1_centered, full_matrices=False)\n    U2, S2, V2t = svd(data2_centered, full_matrices=False)\n\n    # Keep enough components to capture variance\n    k = min(min(data1.shape), min(data2.shape), 100)\n    U1 = U1[:, :k]  # (n_samples1, k)\n    S1 = S1[:k]      # (k,)\n    V1t = V1t[:k, :] # (k, n_features)\n    U2 = U2[:, :k]   # (n_samples2, k)\n    S2 = S2[:k]      # (k,)\n    V2t = V2t[:k, :] # (k, n_features)\n\n    # Now compute cross-correlation in the reduced space\n    # We want to compute correlation between the k-dimensional representations\n    # Cross-covariance in reduced space\n    # (U1 * S1).T @ (U2 * S2) would be (k, n_samples1) @ (n_samples2, k) - WRONG!\n    # We should use: (U1 * S1).T @ (U2 * S2)\
    \ BUT need matching sample dimensions\n    # Instead, work in feature space: V1t @ V2t.T gives (k, k)\n    C12_reduced = V1t @ V2t.T  # (k, n_features) @ (n_features, k) = (k, k)\n\n    # SVD of cross-covariance\n    U_cca, S_cca, Vt_cca = svd(C12_reduced, full_matrices=False)\n\n    # Keep top n_cca components\n    n_cca = min(n_cca, len(S_cca), k)\n    U_cca = U_cca[:, :n_cca]      # (k, n_cca)\n    Vt_cca = Vt_cca[:n_cca, :]    # (n_cca, k)\n\n    # Map back to original feature space\n    # w1 projects from original features to canonical space\n    w1 = V1t.T @ U_cca       # (n_features, k) @ (k, n_cca) = (n_features, n_cca)\n    w2 = V2t.T @ Vt_cca.T    # (n_features, k) @ (k, n_cca) = (n_features, n_cca)\n\n    return w1, w2, S_cca[:n_cca]\n\ndef eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n    print(\"CCA Correction: Starting...\")\n\n    # === STAGE 1: Preprocessing ===\n    print(\"  [Stage 1] Normalizing and selecting HVGs...\")\n    sc.pp.normalize_total(adata,\
    \ target_sum=1e4)\n    sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n    # Using batch_hvg is crucial for CCA to focus on shared signals\n    if 'batch_hvg' in adata.var.columns:\n        adata = adata[:, adata.var['batch_hvg']].copy()\n\n    # === STAGE 2: Iterative Pairwise Alignment ===\n    print(\"  [Stage 2] Performing iterative pairwise CCA alignment...\")\n\n    batch_labels = adata.obs['batch']\n    unique_batches = batch_labels.unique().tolist()\n\n    # Use the largest batch as the reference\n    ref_batch_name = batch_labels.value_counts().idxmax()\n    unique_batches.remove(ref_batch_name)\n\n    # Store corrected data in a new layer\n    adata.layers['corrected_X'] = adata.X.copy()\n\n    ref_data = adata[adata.obs['batch'] == ref_batch_name, :].X\n    if issparse(ref_data): ref_data = ref_data.toarray()\n\n    for query_batch_name in unique_batches:\n        print(f\"    Aligning '{query_batch_name}' to reference...\")\n        query_mask = adata.obs['batch'] == query_batch_name\n        query_data\
    \ = adata[query_mask, :].X\n        if issparse(query_data): query_data = query_data.toarray()\n\n        # Perform CCA\n        w_ref, w_query, _ = _cca(ref_data, query_data, n_cca=30)\n\n        # Project query data into shared space and then back out\n        # This transforms query data to align with reference data\n        aligned_query_data = (query_data @ w_query @ w_ref.T)\n\n        # Put the aligned data back\n        if issparse(adata.layers['corrected_X']):\n            adata.layers['corrected_X'] = csc_matrix(adata.layers['corrected_X'])\n            adata.layers['corrected_X'][query_mask, :] = aligned_query_data\n            adata.layers['corrected_X'] = adata.layers['corrected_X'].tocsr()\n        else:\n            adata.layers['corrected_X'][query_mask, :] = aligned_query_data\n\n    print(\"  [Stage 2] All batches aligned.\")\n\n    # === STAGE 3: Final PCA ===\n    print(\"  [Stage 3] Computing final PCA on aligned expression matrix...\")\n    adata.X = adata.layers['corrected_X']\n\
    \    sc.pp.pca(adata, n_comps=50)\n\n    # === STAGE 4: Create Output ===\n    output = ad.AnnData(\n        obs=adata.obs[[]], var=adata.var[[]],\n        obsm={'X_emb': adata.obsm['X_pca'].astype(np.float32)},\n        uns={'method_id': 'cca_correction'}\n    )\n\n    print(f\"✓ CCA Correction complete.\")\n    return output\n\n```\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: python storage/system/main.py\nLoading training dataset...\n  Data shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Batches: 4 batches\nFound eliminate_batch_effect_fn, executing...\n\n=== Running batch integration ===\nCCA Correction: Starting...\n  [Stage 1] Normalizing and selecting HVGs...\n  [Stage 2] Performing iterative pairwise CCA alignment...\n    Aligning 'Tucker et al. 2020' to reference...\n    Aligning 'Koenig et al. 2022' to reference...\n    Aligning 'Kuppe et al. 2022' to reference...\n  [Stage 2] All batches aligned.\n  [Stage 3] Computing final PCA on aligned expression matrix...\n\
    ✓ CCA Correction complete.\n  Result shape: (20000, 1488)\n  Has embedding: True\n  Has graph: False\n\nResult saved to: storage/shared/tmp/01d44ba8-31b2-422c-bb30-7fba54c3e41a/result_human.h5ad\n\nRESULT_UUID: 01d44ba8-31b2-422c-bb30-7fba54c3e41a\n\n\nSTDERR:\n/home/ubuntu/miniconda3/envs/batch_integration/lib/python3.11/site-packages/scipy/sparse/_index.py:151: SparseEfficiencyWarning: Changing the sparsity structure of a csc_matrix is expensive. lil_matrix is more efficient.\n  self._set_arrayXarray(i, j, x)\n\n```\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nDo not give up on an approach too easily. Implementation details are often the key—tweaking hyperparameters and other specifics may be enough to make an existing method work effectively.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\nNo action received in the last turn.\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You remained in the Research Counter.\n\n**Latest Room\
    \ Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Single-cell RNA-seq Batch Integration |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score | ARI | ASW_batch | ASW_label | Cell_cycle | Graph_conn | NMI | PCR | cLISI | iLISI | kBET |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1098 | 1 | Canonical Correlation Analysis (CCA) based Correction | Axiom I | 714 | 0.5005 | 0.683 | 0.528 | 0.220 | 0.311 | 0.880 | 0.696 | 0.009 | 0.988 | 0.063 | 0.628 |\n| 1095 | 1 | Standardization with Variance Overscaling (1.1x) | Axiom I | 712 | 0.6174 | 0.587 | 0.648 | 0.220 | 0.368 | 0.873 | 0.684 | 1.000 | 0.991 | 0.023 | 0.780 |\n| 1092 | 1 | MNN Correction with Gaussian Kernel Smoothing | Axiom I | 710 | 0.5769 | 0.687 | 0.561 | 0.236 | 0.375 | 0.874 | 0.724 | 0.234 | 0.991 | 0.173\
    \ | 0.915 |\n| 1091 | 1 | Hybrid v4: Latent Standardization + MNN Residual Correction | Axiom I | 709 | 0.4697 | 0.411 | 0.538 | 0.216 | 0.368 | 0.830 | 0.629 | 0.000 | 0.990 | 0.000 | 0.716 |\n| 1089 | 1 | MNN-based Correction in Latent Space | Axiom I | 707 | 0.5728 | 0.757 | 0.450 | 0.224 | 0.366 | 0.860 | 0.702 | 0.277 | 0.993 | 0.277 | 0.823 |\n| 1087 | 1 | Hybrid v3: Latent Standardization + BBKNN Graph | Axiom I | 706 | 0.1759 | 0.117 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.210 | 0.431 | 0.000 | 1.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 |\n| 1085 | 1 | Latent Space Standardization (Mean and Variance Correction) | Axiom I | 705 | 0.6173 | 0.589 | 0.648 | 0.220 | 0.368 | 0.873 | 0.685 | 1.000 | 0.991 | 0.023 | 0.776 |\n| 1082 | 1 | Iterative Centroid Alignment in PCA Space | Axiom I | 703 | 0.5787 | 0.462 | 0.624 | 0.221 | 0.348 | 0.888 | 0.671 | 0.979 | 0.991 | 0.006 | 0.599 |\n| 1080 | 1 | Hybrid v2: PCA Centroid Alignment + BBKNN Graph | Axiom I | 702 | 0.1760 | 0.117 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 | 0.199 | 0.444\
    \ | 0.000 | 1.000 | 0.000 | 0.000 |\n| 1078 | 1 | Latent Space Correction via PCA Centroid Alignment | Axiom I | 701 | 0.5751 | 0.433 | 0.623 | 0.219 | 0.348 | 0.877 | 0.659 | 1.000 | 0.990 | 0.004 | 0.599 |\n\nPage 1 of 2 (sorted by: id)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-2, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Research Counter\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help research}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
