thinking_content: "**The Ensemble Correction Breakthrough**\n\nOkay, this reflection has been incredibly productive. I've completely reframed the problem.  I started with this misguided notion of a monolithic batch effect and now I'm here, at this elegant solution.  The core concept is this: **ensemble correction**.  It's a way to leverage the best of both worlds. The fundamental flaw in my previous approaches was trying to force a single, perfect embedding out of imperfect methods. Standardization provided a globally coherent space but destroyed local structure, MNN Smoothing preserved local relationships but failed to capture global trends. I realized I was trying to *derive* a truth where both were just flawed estimations.\n\nThis is the key insight: I need to fuse these estimations. I need to take the globally accurate embedding from Standardization and blend it with the locally accurate MNN-smoothed embedding. My hypothesis is that a weighted average – a linear combination – will yield\
