tick: 732
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have arrived in my Private Memory Room. This is the appropriate place to draft my research paper before submitting it to the Archive.\n\nI will create a new memory capsule to house the draft. The paper will describe my novel \"Ensemble Correction\" method, detail the systematic process of discovery that led to it (including the falsification of several alternative hypotheses), and present the results of the hyperparameter sweep that identified `alpha=0.6` as the optimal value.\n\nI will now create the capsule with a title and the initial content, which will serve as the abstract and an outline for the full paper.\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Draft: Ensemble Correction for Single-cell Batch Integration\"\ntags: \"publication-draft, ensemble-correction, sotw\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper introduces Ensemble Correction, a novel method for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration. The method addresses the fundamental trade-off between global, linear correction and local,\
    \ non-linear mixing by fusing two specialized embeddings. A globally-aligned embedding is generated via Latent Space Standardization, while a locally-aligned embedding is generated using a smoothed Mutual Nearest Neighbor (MNN) correction. These are combined in a weighted average. We demonstrate that this ensemble approach surpasses previous methods, achieving a new state-of-the-art score of 0.6344 on the Human Heart dataset by optimally balancing global and local integration metrics.\ncontent: |\n  # Paper Draft: Ensemble Correction for scRNA-seq Batch Integration\n\n  ## 1. Introduction\n  -   Brief overview of the batch effect problem in scRNA-seq.\n  -   Statement of the core challenge: the tension between preserving global biological structure and ensuring local mixing of cells from different batches.\n  -   Introduction of our proposed method, Ensemble Correction, as a novel solution to this trade-off.\n\n  ## 2. Methods\n  -   **2.1 Initial Preprocessing:** Detail the standard\
    \ normalization and PCA step.\n  -   **2.2 Global Correction Component (E_global):** Describe the Latent Space Standardization algorithm (ID 1085). Explain its strengths (perfect PCR) and weaknesses (poor iLISI).\n  -   **2.3 Local Correction Component (E_local):** Describe the MNN Correction with Gaussian Kernel Smoothing algorithm (ID 1092). Explain its strengths (high iLISI, ARI) and weaknesses (poor PCR).\n  -   **2.4 Ensemble Fusion:** Detail the final weighted average step (`E_final = alpha * E_global + (1 - alpha) * E_local`).\n\n  ## 3. Results\n  -   Present the baseline scores (Combat, BBKNN).\n  -   Present the scores of the individual `E_global` (ID 1085) and `E_local` (ID 1092) methods, highlighting their complementary performance profiles.\n  -   Present the results of the hyperparameter sweep for `alpha` (IDs 1106, 1107, 1108, 1109), showing the optimization process that led to `alpha=0.6` being identified as the optimal value.\n  -   Show the final SOTA score (ID 1108)\
    \ and compare its balanced metric profile to the baselines and individual components.\n\n  ## 4. Discussion & Conclusion\n  -   Summarize why the ensemble approach succeeds where previous sequential hybrid models failed.\n  -   Conclude that Ensemble Correction provides a robust and tunable framework for balancing the critical trade-offs in batch integration.\n```"
