tick: 737
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am back in the Private Memory Room. My research has reached a stable conclusion for now, and my focus shifts to documenting my findings. I have a draft capsule (ID 1) ready.\n\nI will now update this capsule by filling out the content based on the outline I created. This will involve detailing the methods and presenting the key results from my series of experiments, culminating in my SOTA submission (ID 1108). This expanded text will form the body of the paper I intend to publish.\n\n/execute_action{update 1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Paper: Ensemble Correction for Single-cell Batch Integration\n\n  ## Abstract\n  This paper introduces Ensemble Correction, a novel method for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration. The method addresses the fundamental trade-off between global, linear correction and local, non-linear mixing by fusing two specialized embeddings. A globally-aligned embedding is generated via Latent Space Standardization, while a locally-aligned embedding is generated\
    \ using a smoothed Mutual Nearest Neighbor (MNN) correction. These are combined in a weighted average. We demonstrate through systematic experimentation that this ensemble approach surpasses previous methods, achieving a new state-of-the-art score of 0.6344 on the Human Heart dataset by optimally balancing global and local integration metrics.\n\n  ## 1. Introduction\n  The analysis of single-cell RNA-sequencing (scRNA-seq) data is often complicated by batch effects, which are technical variations that can obscure true biological signals. A central challenge in correcting for these effects is the inherent tension between two objectives: 1) aligning the global distributions of batches to remove large-scale technical variation, and 2) interleaving cells from different batches at a local level to ensure that similar cell types are neighbors in the corrected space. Methods that excel at one objective often fail at the other. We observed this trade-off in our own experiments, where methods\
    \ providing excellent global correction (measured by PCR) had poor local mixing (measured by iLISI), and vice versa. To resolve this, we propose Ensemble Correction, a framework that fuses two independent, specialized embeddings to achieve a superior balance.\n\n  ## 2. Methods\n\n  ### 2.1 Initial Preprocessing\n  All experiments begin with a standard preprocessing pipeline. Raw UMI counts are normalized to a target sum of 10,000 per cell, followed by a log1p transformation. Principal Component Analysis (PCA) is then performed to generate a 50-dimensional latent space representation, which serves as the input for all subsequent correction methods.\n\n  ### 2.2 Global Correction Component (E_global)\n  The first component, `E_global`, is designed for global coherence. It is generated using our **Latent Space Standardization** method (ID 1085, Score: 0.6173). For each principal component, this algorithm standardizes the cell coordinates within each batch (subtracting the batch mean, dividing\
    \ by the batch standard deviation) and then rescales them to match the global mean and standard deviation. This forces the statistical moments of each batch to match the global distribution, resulting in a perfect Principal Component Regression (PCR) score of 1.0, but a very low local mixing score (iLISI of 0.023).\n\n  ### 2.3 Local Correction Component (E_local)\n  The second component, `E_local`, is designed for local accuracy. It is generated using our **MNN Correction with Gaussian Kernel Smoothing** method (ID 1092, Score: 0.5769). This algorithm first identifies Mutual Nearest Neighbor (MNN) pairs between batches in the original PCA space. These pairs act as \"anchors\" to estimate the batch effect vector field. A smooth correction field is then generated by interpolating these anchor vectors across all cells using a Gaussian kernel-weighted average of their `k` nearest anchors. This results in excellent local mixing (iLISI of 0.173) and clustering (ARI of 0.687), but it distorts\
    \ the global structure, leading to a very poor PCR score (0.234).\n\n  ### 2.4 Ensemble Fusion\n  The final embedding, `E_final`, is a linear combination of the two specialized embeddings:\n  `E_final = alpha * E_global + (1 - alpha) * E_local`\n  The `alpha` parameter serves as a hyperparameter to control the trade-off between global and local correction.\n\n  ## 3. Results\n  We performed a systematic parameter sweep to identify the optimal value for `alpha`. The results demonstrate a clear performance peak:\n  -   `alpha=0.8` (ID 1106): Score 0.6270 (PCR: 0.973, iLISI: 0.062)\n  -   `alpha=0.7` (ID 1107): Score 0.6291 (PCR: 0.936, iLISI: 0.086)\n  -   **`alpha=0.6` (ID 1108): Score 0.6344 (PCR: 0.884, iLISI: 0.120) - SOTA**\n  -   `alpha=0.5` (ID 1109): Score 0.6340 (PCR: 0.815, iLISI: 0.161)\n\n  The optimal performance was achieved with `alpha=0.6`, establishing a new state-of-the-art score. This configuration successfully balances the two objectives, retaining a strong global correction\
    \ (PCR of 0.884) while significantly improving the local mixing (iLISI of 0.120) compared to any other high-PCR method.\n\n  ## 4. Discussion & Conclusion\n  Our work demonstrates that the conflict between global and local batch correction can be effectively resolved through an ensemble approach. By computing globally- and locally-optimized embeddings independently and then fusing them, we avoid the destructive interference observed in sequential hybrid methods. The Ensemble Correction framework provides a robust, interpretable, and high-performing solution to the scRNA-seq batch integration problem.\n```"
