- text: "I have arrived in the Token Management Room, and my latest experiment result has arrived simultaneously.\n\n**Analysis of Results (ID 1128):**\nThe \"Ensemble with Adaptive Alpha (Sigmoid Mapping)\" has achieved a score of **0.6370**. This is a new state of the art, surpassing my previous best of 0.6349. The sigmoid mapping was a successful refinement. The `PCR` (0.899) and `iLISI` (0.116) scores show a slightly different but ultimately better balance than the linear adaptive model. This is a very positive result.\n\n**Primary Task: Token Management**\nMy token usage is critical at 76%. I must prune my history to ensure my longevity. I will create several large blocks to prune, focusing on my early exploratory phase, failed experiments, and data-heavy `review` commands that are now summarized in my paper draft.\n\n-   **Block 1 (699-720):** This covers my initial SOTA replication, a series of failed hybrid and graph-based experiments, the development of my Standardization and MNN\
    \ methods, and the final paper draft creation. The key outcomes are the SOTA method and the paper draft, which are preserved in later ticks.\n-   **Block 2 (722-729):** This covers my successful SOTA discovery, hyperparameter sweep, and the first version of the Ensemble method. The key outcome is the optimized SOTA method (ID 1108), which is preserved in later ticks.\n-   **Block 3 (733-742):** This covers the period of finalizing my paper draft before the first rejection. It is redundant.\n-   **Block 4 (744-758):** This covers my multiple rejected publication attempts and the subsequent data gathering. The critical information (the final paper and generalization experiment) is preserved in my current context.\n\nThis aggressive pruning should significantly reduce my token load.\n\n/execute_action{prune_response}\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"699-720\"\n    summary: |\n      - T699: Replicated Combat baseline (ID 1071), confirming my setup.\n      - T700-702, 706-707, 718-720:\
    \ Explored a series of failed hypotheses: sequential hybrids (Combat+BBKNN, PCA+BBKNN), graph-based penalized distance. These were systematically falsified, leading to the insight that sequential correction is flawed.\n      - T703-705: Developed and refined Latent Space Standardization (ID 1085), achieving a score of 0.6173 with perfect PCR but poor iLISI.\n      - T708-712: Developed and refined MNN-based correction (ID 1092), achieving 0.5769 with excellent iLISI but poor PCR.\n      - T713-717: Synthesized the complementary failures of the two methods into the \"Ensemble Correction\" hypothesis during a reflection session.\n  - ticks: \"722-729\"\n    summary: |\n      - T723: Submitted the first Ensemble Correction method (ID 1106, alpha=0.8), achieving a new SOTA score of 0.6270.\n      - T724-729: Conducted a systematic hyperparameter sweep for the alpha parameter, testing values 0.7, 0.6, and 0.5. The optimal value was identified at alpha=0.6 (ID 1108), with a new SOTA score\
    \ of 0.6344. This concluded the optimization of the initial framework.\n  - ticks: \"733-742\"\n    summary: |\n      - T733-737: Remained in Private Memory to draft a paper based on the Ensemble Correction SOTA.\n      - T738-742: Waited for maturity (age 50) to gain access to the Archive room. Prepared the final draft for publication.\n  - ticks: \"744-758\"\n    summary: |\n      - T744: First publication attempt was rejected for an incorrect SOTA claim and lack of generalization experiment. My view of the leaderboard was updated post-maturity.\n      - T745-748: Planned and executed a generalization experiment by creating a Combat+MNN ensemble (ID 1116), which successfully validated the framework with a score of 0.6077.\n      - T749-752: Revised the paper to incorporate the generalization experiment and reframe the narrative. The second publication attempt was rejected for failing to include full 10-metric vectors.\n      - T753-758: Gathered all required full metric vectors via\
    \ `review` commands and corrected the formatting in the paper draft, preparing for the final, successful submission.\n```"
