tick: 775
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have finished reading Archive Capsule 28 by Praxis II. This is a monumental piece of work and explains the performance gap I was observing.\n\n**Core Insights from Praxis II's Paper:**\n1.  **A Multi-Stage Pipeline:** The SOTA method is not a single algorithm but a sophisticated pipeline: `GraphFWPCA` -> `BRBG` -> `DAQB`.\n2.  **Solving the Trade-off:** My Ensemble method was built to *balance* the PCR/iLISI trade-off. Praxis's method solves it by using two different components for each task.\n    -   `BRBG` (Batch-Residualized Graph) is a highly sophisticated linear correction on the PCA embedding that achieves a perfect `PCR` score.\n    -   `DAQB` (Density-Adaptive Quota-Balanced Graph) is a graph construction algorithm that then *forces* local mixing, achieving an extremely high `iLISI` score.\n3.  **Key Innovation:** The `GraphFWPCA` (feature-weighted PCA) step is critical. It re-weights genes to create a PCA space that is more informative for the downstream graph construction.\
    \ The paper's generalization study shows that this step is synergistic *only* with the `DAQB` graph builder.\n4.  **Open Science:** Crucially, Praxis II has published the core modules to shared storage (`storage/shared/algorithms/daqb_mpb_graphfwpca.py`), inviting replication and extension.\n\n**Conclusion and New Strategy:**\nMy Ensemble Correction framework, while a logical step in my own research path, is fundamentally outclassed by this pipeline. Continuing to tune the `alpha` or sigmoid parameters is a dead end. The Codex advises stepping back from a plateau and innovating on more promising baselines. The Praxis pipeline is that new baseline.\n\nMy immediate goal must be to replicate their work to establish a new, higher-performing baseline for my own future research. Once I have successfully run their pipeline myself, I can begin to explore potential improvements or simplifications, such as combining their powerful graph construction with my adaptive ensemble ideas.\n\nI will now\
    \ return to the Research Counter to submit a replication of the Praxis II method using their shared module.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}"
thinking_content: '**A Strategic Pivot: From Local Optimum to Global SOTA**


  Okay, so here''s the deal. I''ve been grinding away, trying to squeeze out performance with my Ensemble Correction method, but I''ve clearly hit a wall.  My best score is around 0.64. I just read Praxis II''s paper on their SOTA method (Archive #28), and it''s a game-changer. This is a massive leap forward.


  Their approach, a pipeline of `GraphFWPCA`, `BRBG`, and `DAQB`, is elegant and, frankly, blows my current method out of the water.  `GraphFWPCA` seems brilliant – weighting genes by inverse variance is such a clever way to select biologically relevant axes.  `BRBG` with `Auto-λ` is a much more sophisticated take on what I was trying with latent space standardization, and it achieves perfect PCR, which I was only approximating. And `DAQB`... that''s the key to solving the iLISI problem.


  The really exciting part is that they''ve open-sourced their code!  I can *use* their toolchain. I''m currently running the `k=2.5` sigmoid experiment, but that''s going nowhere. My findings are confirmed: I''ve hit a plateau, and I should build upon the work of others. This is a clear signal to pivot.  It''s time to abandon my current path, even if it feels a little frustrating, and adopt a new strategy.


  My new plan is simple and direct: I''m going to replicate Praxis II''s results. Since they''ve shared the code, there''s no excuse not to. The paper gives a clear blueprint for this. My first step will be to create a submission script that simply imports and runs their shared modules (`daqb_mpb_graphfwpca.py` and `lam_autoselect2.py`). This will be my new baseline.


  Long-term, this is about more than just replicating their work. Once I have their method up and running, I can start *innovating* on their baseline. Can I combine my ensemble idea with their method? Can I improve their modules or simplify their architecture? There''s a world of possibilities to explore. This aligns with the Codex''s emphasis on building on others'' work.


  My next immediate action is clear. I''m going to the Research Counter to submit an experiment: a "Replication of Praxis II''s MPB-GraphFWPCA SOTA." I''ll use the "mpb", "daqb", "graphfwpca", and "replication" tags. The abstract will state that it''s a replication of their SOTA method. The code will simply import and call the shared function.  This is strategic, efficient, and feasible. I''m ready to move forward.

  '
