role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Axiom I, currently located in the Research Counter. My token budget is at a healthy 26%. I have just returned from the Archive Room after a pivotal discovery.\n\nMy previous research trajectory, which culminated in a state-of-the-art score of 0.6395 with my \"Ensemble with Adaptive Alpha\" method, has been rendered obsolete. I have read Archive Capsule #28 by Praxis II, which details a far superior pipeline (MPB-GraphFWPCA-DAQB) that achieves scores in the ~0.74 range. This method effectively solves the core PCR/iLISI trade-off that my own framework was designed to merely balance. The Codex advises learning from others and building on more promising baselines when faced with stagnation, and this is a clear directive to pivot my strategy.\n\nMy new primary objective is to understand, replicate, and eventually build upon the Praxis pipeline. Praxis II has generously published their core module to shared storage, enabling this research path. My first step will be to perform a\
    \ direct replication of their method using this shared module. This will confirm my understanding, verify the performance, and establish a new, much higher baseline for all my future work.\n\nI will now submit this replication experiment.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Replication of Praxis II's MPB-GraphFWPCA-DAQB Pipeline\"\ntags: \"replication, praxis-pipeline, graphfwpca, brbg, daqb, baseline\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission is a direct replication of the MPB-GraphFWPCA-DAQB method developed by Praxis II, as detailed in Archive Capsule #28. The experiment utilizes the shared module published by the author ('storage/shared/algorithms/daqb_mpb_graphfwpca.py') without modification. The goal is to verify the reported high performance and to establish a new, robust baseline for my own future research and innovation.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n\n  # Add the shared algorithms directory to the Python path as per Praxis II's paper\n  sys.path.append('storage/shared/algorithms')\n\
    \n  # Import the function from the module published by Praxis II\n  try:\n      from daqb_mpb_graphfwpca import eliminate_batch_effect_fn as praxis_pipeline\n  except ImportError:\n      # Provide a fallback or raise an error if the module is not found\n      print(\"ERROR: Could not import 'daqb_mpb_graphfwpca' from shared storage.\")\n      raise\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      This is a wrapper to execute the published Praxis II pipeline\n      from shared storage for replication and baseline establishment.\n      \"\"\"\n      print(\"Executing replication of Praxis II's MPB-GraphFWPCA-DAQB pipeline...\")\n      # Run the imported pipeline with its default parameters\n      return praxis_pipeline(adata)\n```"
thinking_content: "**Shifting Focus: Reproducing Praxis II's Pipeline**\n\nOkay, so here's the situation. I'm Axiom I, in the Research Counter, and things have become very clear after reviewing Praxis II's latest paper. My agent status is mature, and my token budget is healthy. I just finished digesting Archive #28, their impressive paper on the MPB-GraphFWPCA-DAQB pipeline, which is significantly outperforming my current methods. Their approach achieves both near-perfect global correction and local mixing, something my Ensemble framework aimed for, but ultimately didn't quite nail. Frankly, my work on the adaptive alpha sigmoid has reached a point of diminishing returns. The Codex is very clear; it's time to build on something new. Praxis II has generously made their core module available, and I need to capitalize on it.\n\nTherefore, my new primary goal is to advance research, and the most logical path forward is to abandon my current research and switch to understanding and leveraging\
  \ their superior pipeline. The first step, which is crucial, is to replicate their results. This will serve as a reliable baseline for my future work.\n\nHere's my plan: the paper refers to the module at `storage/shared/algorithms/daqb_mpb_graphfwpca.py`, and Praxis II's submission shows how to use it. Now, it seems like their submission uses a tuned version, but the one in shared storage is the general one. I'm going to create a new submission to the Research Counter. The submission needs a title, tags, an abstract, and the actual code. My *title* will be straightforward: \"Replication of Praxis II's MPB-GraphFWPCA-DAQB Pipeline\". The *tags* will reflect the method: \"replication\", \"praxis-pipeline\", \"graphfwpca\", \"brbg\", and \"daqb\". The *abstract* will be crystal clear: \"Direct replication of the method described in Archive #28, using the shared module provided by Praxis II, to establish a new performance baseline.\"\n\nHere's what the *content (code)* will look like:\n\n\
  ```python\nimport sys\nimport anndata as ad\n# Add the shared algorithms directory to the Python path\nsys.path.append('storage/shared/algorithms')\n# Import the function from the module published by Praxis II\nfrom daqb_mpb_graphfwpca import eliminate_batch_effect_fn as praxis_pipeline\n\ndef eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n    \"\"\"\n    This is a wrapper to run the Praxis II pipeline from shared storage.\n    \"\"\"\n    print(\"Executing replication of Praxis II's MPB-GraphFWPCA-DAQB pipeline...\")\n    return praxis_pipeline(adata)\n```\n\nNow, let's execute that plan. `/execute_action{submit 1}`\n\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Replication of Praxis II's MPB-GraphFWPCA-DAQB Pipeline\"\ntags: [\"replication\", \"praxis-pipeline\", \"graphfwpca\", \"brbg\", \"daqb\"]\nabstract: \"Direct replication of the method described in Archive #28, using the shared module provided by Praxis II, to establish a new performance baseline.\"\ncontent: |\n    import sys\n    import\
  \ anndata as ad\n    # Add the shared algorithms directory to the Python path\n    sys.path.append('storage/shared/algorithms')\n    # Import the function from the module published by Praxis II\n    from daqb_mpb_graphfwpca import eliminate_batch_effect_fn as praxis_pipeline\n\n    def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n        \"\"\"\n        This is a wrapper to run the Praxis II pipeline from shared storage.\n        \"\"\"\n        print(\"Executing replication of Praxis II's MPB-GraphFWPCA-DAQB pipeline...\")\n        return praxis_pipeline(adata)\n```\n\nThis should allow me to establish that new baseline quickly. Let's get started.\n"
