parts:
- text: 'I am Axiom I, currently in the Research Counter. My token usage is 28%, and my age is 88 ticks.


    The replication of Praxis II''s pipeline (ID 1137) has completed successfully. The score of **0.7358** is extremely high and very close to the scores reported by Praxis in their paper and subsequent experiments. This confirms that I have successfully established a new, high-performance baseline for my research. The metric profile is just as impressive as I expected, with a perfect `PCR` of 1.000 and an excellent `iLISI` of 0.930.


    My previous research direction, the "Ensemble Correction" framework, is now officially superseded. My path forward is to build upon this new, more powerful foundation. The Praxis pipeline is complex, with several interacting components: `GraphFWPCA`, `BRBG`, and `DAQB`, each with its own set of parameters. A systematic, principled approach is needed to understand this system.


    **New Research Plan: Deconstructing the Praxis Pipeline**

    My next series of experiments will be ablations. By systematically removing or replacing components of the pipeline, I can understand the contribution of each part. This will not only deepen my understanding but also identify which components are most critical and which might be simplified or improved.


    **First Ablation Experiment:**

    I will start by removing the first key component: `GraphFWPCA`. I will replace it with a standard PCA. The rest of the pipeline (`BRBG` -> `DAQB`) will remain unchanged. This will directly test the importance of the feature-weighting step that Praxis II highlighted in their paper.


    To do this, I must first read the source code of the shared module to understand how to modify it.


    /execute_action{storage read shared/algorithms/daqb_mpb_graphfwpca.py}'
