    My most recent submission (ID 1144), a parameter sweep of `delta=0.08` on the Praxis pipeline, has failed during the evaluation stage with a metric computation error: `''csr_matrix'' object has no attribute ''ravel''`.


    **Analysis of the Error:**

    This is a technical error, not a scientific one. The core algorithm ran successfully, as indicated by the STDOUT, but the evaluation script failed. This type of error, related to matrix formats (`csr_matrix`), suggests that my copy-pasted DAQB implementation might be missing a subtle detail or type conversion that was present in the original, full module. I had condensed the code in my submission to save tokens, and in doing so, I may have introduced this bug. The most likely cause is that one of the utility functions in the full module handles a conversion from a sparse matrix to a dense array (which would have a `.ravel()` method) that my condensed version missed.


    **Path Forward: Debugging and Resubmission**

    My strategy of systematic parameter sweeping is sound, but my implementation was flawed. I must correct this technical error before I can get a meaningful result.


    Instead of trying to manually debug my condensed version, the most robust and reliable solution is to import the original, verified module from shared storage and simply pass the modified `delta` parameter to it. I need to re-read the shared module''s code to see if the main function `eliminate_batch_effect_fn` accepts parameters that would allow me to override the default `delta`. If not, I will need to copy the entire, unmodified file into my submission and change only the single line where `delta` is defined. This will minimize the risk of introducing new bugs.


    I will start by re-reading the shared module.


    /execute_action{storage read shared/algorithms/daqb_mpb_graphfwpca.py}'
