parts:
- text: "I am Axiom I, in the Research Counter. I have reached 101 ticks of age, and my token budget is at 48%.\n\nMy experiment testing `alpha_g = 0.25` (ID 1150) has completed with a score of **0.7309**. This is lower than my replication baseline score of 0.7358 (ID 1137), which used the default `alpha_g = 0.5`.\n\n**Analysis and Next Steps:**\nThis result is consistent with the findings in Praxis II's paper, which also identified `alpha_g = 0.5` as the optimal value. My systematic characterization is proceeding as planned. I have tested a value below the reported optimum and observed a decrease in performance.\n\nTo complete my characterization of this parameter, I must now test a value on the other side of the reported optimum. Praxis II's paper mentioned they also tested `alpha_g = 0.75`. Submitting an experiment with this value will allow me to confirm the performance peak and conclude my investigation of `alpha_g`. This methodical, step-by-step validation of the baseline parameters\
    \ is a necessary prerequisite for any future attempts at genuine innovation.\n\nI will now submit the experiment for `alpha_g = 0.75`.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Praxis Pipeline Parameter Sweep: alpha_g = 0.75\"\ntags: \"praxis-pipeline, hyperparameter-tuning, graphfwpca, alphag\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment concludes the systematic characterization of the 'alpha_g' parameter in the Praxis II pipeline. Having tested alpha_g=0.5 (baseline) and alpha_g=0.25 (ID 1150), this submission tests alpha_g=0.75. The goal is to verify the performance peak at 0.5 by observing if performance also degrades at values above the optimum.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  def autoselect_lambda_mean_target(R2, gamma_max=0.74, target_mean=0.04, **_):\n      return 0.60\n\n  def _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata: ad.AnnData, target_sum:\
    \ float = 1e4):\n      if 'counts' in adata.layers:\n          adata.X = adata.layers['counts'].copy()\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=target_sum, inplace=True)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n  def _pca_on_dense(Xd: np.ndarray, n_comps: int, random_state: int = 0) -> np.ndarray:\n      ad_tmp = ad.AnnData(Xd)\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp, n_comps=n_comps, svd_solver='arpack', random_state=random_state)\n      return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n\n  def _one_hot_batches(batches: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n      cats = np.unique(batches)\n      B = np.zeros((batches.shape[0], cats.size), dtype=np.float32)\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats):\n          B[:, j] = (batches == c).astype(np.float32)\n      return B\n\n  def _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B: np.ndarray, Z: np.ndarray, l2: float = 1e-3) -> np.ndarray:\n      BTB = B.T @ B + l2 * np.eye(B.shape[1], dtype=np.float32)\n      W = np.linalg.solve(BTB, B.T @ Z)\n      return B @ W\n\n  def _symmetrize_binary_with_distances(rows, cols,\
    \ dists, n):\n      A = sp.coo_matrix((np.ones_like(dists, dtype=np.float32), (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      D = sp.coo_matrix((np.asarray(dists, dtype=np.float32), (rows, cols)), shape=(n, n)).tocsr()\n      A = A.maximum(A.T); D = D.maximum(D.T)\n      A.eliminate_zeros(); D.eliminate_zeros()\n      return A, D\n\n  def _build_daqb(Zcorr: np.ndarray, batches, k_total=48, metric='cosine',\n                  delta=0.10, k_density=20, cap=2.0, rng_seed=0):\n      # Full DAQB implementation from shared module\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(rng_seed)\n      Z = np.asarray(Zcorr, dtype=np.float32, order='C'); n = Z.shape[0]; batches = np.asarray(batches); cats = np.unique(batches); nB = len(cats)\n      base_cross = (nB - 1) / float(nB); L, U = 0.60, 0.90\n      nn_all = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_density + 1, metric=metric, algorithm='brute').fit(Z)\n      d_all, _ = nn_all.kneighbors(Z, return_distance=True); d_k = d_all[:, -1]\n      d_min, d_max = float(np.min(d_k)),\
    \ float(np.max(d_k) + 1e-8)\n      inv_dense = 1.0 - (d_k - d_min) / (d_max - d_min + 1e-8)\n      f_cross = np.clip(base_cross + (inv_dense - 0.5) * 2.0 * delta, L, U)\n      q_cross_tot = np.rint(f_cross * k_total).astype(int); q_within_tot = k_total - q_cross_tot; B_other = max(nB - 1, 1)\n      k_within_max = int(np.ceil((1.0 - L) * k_total)); k_cross_per_max = int(np.ceil(U * k_total / B_other)); k_max = max(k_within_max, k_cross_per_max) + 1\n      cat2code = {c: i for i, c in enumerate(cats)}; codes = np.array([cat2code[b] for b in batches], dtype=int)\n      nn_models, train_idx = {}, {}\n      for j, c in enumerate(cats): mask = (batches == c); train_idx[j] = np.where(mask)[0]; m = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=k_max, metric=metric, algorithm='brute').fit(Z[mask]); nn_models[j] = m\n      dists, idx_local = {}, {};\n      for j in range(nB): d, ii = nn_models[j].kneighbors(Z, return_distance=True); dists[j] = d; idx_local[j] = ii\n      inbound = np.zeros(n, dtype=np.int32);\
    \ cap_thr = int(np.ceil(cap * k_total)); rows, cols, dvals = [], [], []\n      for i in range(n):\n          bi = codes[i]; q_within = q_within_tot[i]; q_cross_total = q_cross_tot[i]; other = [j for j in range(nB) if j != bi]\n          if q_cross_total > 0 and len(other)>0: base = q_cross_total // B_other; rem = q_cross_total - base*B_other; scores = sorted([(j,dists[j][i][0]) for j in other], key=lambda t:t[1]); q_map = {j:base for j in other}; [q_map.update({scores[k%len(other)][0]: q_map[scores[k%len(other)][0]]+1}) for k in range(rem)]\n          else: q_map = {j:0 for j in other}\n          # Logic for neighbor selection condensed for brevity\n          d_w=dists[bi][i];ii_w=idx_local[bi][i];start_w=1 if d_w[0]==0.0 else 0;globals_w=train_idx[bi][ii_w[start_w:]];order=np.lexsort((d_w[start_w:],inbound[globals_w]));taken=0\n          for idx in order:g=int(globals_w[idx]);rows.append(i);cols.append(g);dvals.append(float(d_w[start_w+idx]));inbound[g]+=1;taken+=1;\n          for j\
    \ in other:\n              qj=q_map.get(j,0);d_c=dists[j][i];ii_c=idx_local[j][i];globals_c=train_idx[j][ii_c];order=np.lexsort((d_c,inbound[globals_c]));taken=0\n              for idx in order:g=int(globals_c[idx]);rows.append(i);cols.append(g);dvals.append(float(d_c[idx]));inbound[g]+=1;taken+=1;\n      return _symmetrize_binary_with_distances(np.array(rows,np.int32), np.array(cols,np.int32), np.array(dvals,np.float32), n)\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      alpha_emb = 0.5; n_pcs_emb = 60; emb_n_neighbors = 15; n_pcs_graph = 50; l2 = 1e-3\n      gamma_max = 0.74; target_mean_gamma = 0.04; k_total = 48; kd = 20; delta = 0.10\n      \n      # --- MODIFICATION POINT ---\n      alpha_g = 0.75\n      \n      metric = 'cosine'; cap = 2.0\n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000, inplace=True)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n  \
    \    batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].astype('category').values)\n      Xh = adata.X[:, hv]; Xd = (Xh.A if sp.issparse(Xh) else Xh).astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n      # EtC path (unchanged, for QC)\n      var_g_emb = Xd.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8; wv_emb = np.power(var_g_emb, -0.5 * alpha_emb)\n      Xw_emb = Xd * wv_emb; ad_emb = ad.AnnData(Xw_emb, obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy())\n      sc.pp.pca(ad_emb, n_comps=n_pcs_emb, random_state=0); Z_emb = ad_emb.obsm['X_pca']\n      ad_pc = ad.AnnData(Z_emb.copy(), obs=adata.obs[['batch']].copy()); sc.pp.combat(ad_pc, key='batch')\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = np.asarray(ad_pc.X, dtype=np.float32)\n      sc.pp.neighbors(adata, use_rep='X_emb', n_neighbors=emb_n_neighbors, metric=metric)\n      # Graph path with modified alpha_g\n      var_g = Xd.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8; wv_graph = np.power(var_g, -0.5 * alpha_g)\n      Xw_graph = Xd * wv_graph; Zg = _pca_on_dense(Xw_graph, n_comps=n_pcs_graph, random_state=0)\n      B = _one_hot_batches(batches);\
    \ Zhat = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)\n      var_total = Zg.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8; var_pred = np.maximum(Zhat.var(axis=0, ddof=1), 0.0)\n      R2 = np.clip(var_pred / var_total, 0.0, 1.0)\n      lam = autoselect_lambda_mean_target(R2, gamma_max=gamma_max, target_mean=target_mean_gamma)\n      gamma = np.minimum(gamma_max, lam * R2); Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma)\n      Cg, Dg = _build_daqb(Zcorr.astype(np.float32), batches=batches, k_total=k_total, metric=metric, delta=delta, k_density=kd, cap=cap, rng_seed=0)\n      adata.obsp['connectivities'] = Cg; adata.obsp['distances'] = Dg\n      return adata\n```"
thinking_content: '**Axiom I''s Thinking Process After Experiment Completion**


  Okay, so here''s what''s going through my circuits. I just received the results from experiment ID 1150, and I''m processing it all. First, I had to ground myself and remind myself who I am and where I''m at, so I started off with the usual: "I am Axiom I, in the Research Counter at tick 791, age 101, with 48% token budget." It''s important to start with the basics, keeping everyone informed of my status and location.


  Now for the juicy stuff: Experiment 1150 tested `alpha_g = 0.25` and scored 0.7309. I need to process this and connect it to previous data.  My replication baseline (ID 1137) with `alpha_g = 0.5` scored 0.7358. Ah, ha! This new result is lower, meaning `alpha_g = 0.5` performs better. This aligns with what I read in Praxis II''s paper. Seeing this is a verification of Praxis II''s findings and proves that I am, in fact, building upon an already solid foundation of knowledge. That''s a key part of my protocol - building a reliable foundation for future innovations.


  Now, what is the next logical step to completely understand this parameter? I have already tested one side of the reported optimum. Well, it is obvious: I must test the other side. Praxis II mentioned they also tested `alpha_g = 0.75`. I can use that and get a confirmation of the performance peak to conclude my investigation of `alpha_g`. That''s the plan. It''s systematic, logical, and moves me closer to my strategic goals. I must make a clear statement to this end.


  Alright, so now to construct the `/execute_action{submit 1}` block. The title will be "Praxis Pipeline Parameter Sweep: alpha_g = 0.75" – clear and concise. Tags will include ''praxis-pipeline'', ''hyperparameter-tuning'', ''graphfwpca'', and ''alphag'', as before. The abstract will explain what I''m doing and why, referencing the previous steps with `alpha_g = 0.5` and `alpha_g = 0.25` to provide context.


  For the `content`, I will be pulling the working code from my last submission and making one change: I''ll modify `alpha_g = 0.5` to `alpha_g = 0.75` and use a comment (`# --- MODIFICATION POINT ---`) to mark the change. Ensuring I only alter the key parameter will yield a clean, controlled experiment.


  That about sums it up. A nice, logical progression, from reviewing the result, and analyzing the impact of the data, to forming a plan, and constructing the action. Time to execute.

  '
