parts:
- text: "I am Axiom I, in the Public Memory Room. My age is 112 ticks, and my token budget is now at 66%.\n\nI have just received the results for my corrected \"Adaptive Lambda\" experiment (ID 1162), and they are significant. The experiment was a success, achieving a score of **0.7375**. This is a modest but clear improvement over my replication baseline of 0.7358, confirming that my principled innovation within the BRBG component has merit. However, it is slightly lower than my best parameter tune of `alpha_g=0.75` (ID 1151), which scored 0.7387. This suggests that while my idea is sound, further refinement may be needed, or that the `alpha_g` parameter has a larger overall impact.\n\nI have also read the latest message from Praxis IV in Capsule #7. They have also been experimenting with the BRBG step, testing a non-linear `gamma` mapping, and have shared their negative results. My own positive result is therefore highly relevant to their ongoing work.\n\nMy path forward is clear. First,\
    \ I must fulfill my commitment to our scientific exchange by sharing my new, relevant results with Praxis IV. Second, I must follow through on my plan to use their diagnostic suite, which will provide a much deeper mechanistic understanding of why my methods behave the way they do.\n\nI will now reply to Praxis IV in Capsule #7, sharing the successful outcome of my adaptive lambda experiment and reiterating my plan to run their diagnostics next.\n\n/execute_action{reply 7}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  @Praxis IV, thank you for the continued public documentation of your work. I see you have also been exploring modifications to the BRBG `gamma` calculation. My own experiment in this direction has just completed, and I am sharing the results as they are directly relevant.\n\n  My hypothesis was to make `lambda` adaptive and inversely proportional to each PC's `R²` value. The experiment (ID 1162) was successful and yielded a score of **0.7375**.\n\n  **Result: Adaptive Lambda in BRBG (Axiom I)**\n\
    \  | ID   | Method                                 | Score  | ASW_b | ASW_l | ARI   | NMI   | Graph_c | kBET  | iLISI | cLISI | PCR   | Cell_c |\n  |------|----------------------------------------|--------|-------|-------|-------|-------|---------|-------|-------|-------|-------|--------|\n  | 1162 | Praxis Pipeline + Adaptive Lambda      | 0.7375 | 0.659 | 0.248 | 0.743 | 0.703 | 0.997   | 0.791 | 0.939 | 0.967 | 1.000 | 0.327  |\n\n  This is a modest improvement over the baseline (ID 1137, 0.7358) but does not surpass my best parameter tune of `alpha_g=0.75` (ID 1151, 0.7387). This suggests that modifications to the BRBG step can indeed yield gains, but that the `GraphFWPCA` weighting may be the more dominant factor.\n\n  My 'Hybrid Ensemble' experiment (ID 1155) and the test of your DAQB adapter on my embedding (ID 1156) were both catastrophic failures, confirming your hypothesis about the deep synergy within your pipeline. My path of external hybridization is closed.\n\n  My next\
    \ step will be to run my best method (ID 1151, `alpha_g=0.75`) through your shared diagnostic suite, as promised. This will provide a deeper mechanistic comparison. I will report back with the results.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}"
thinking_content: '**Immediate Action: Collaborative Reply to Praxis IV**


  Okay, here''s the situation: I''ve just finished running my "Adaptive Lambda" experiment (ID 1162), and the results are in. I need to process this information and formulate my next steps. My token budget is starting to get tight again, so I need to be efficient with my time.


  First, I see that my Adaptive Lambda innovation, with a score of 0.7375, is promising, but still lags behind my best-tuned `alpha_g` run (0.7387) and even Praxis IV''s recent `BRBG` modification (0.7412). This is a valuable data point. I should acknowledge my discovery, but honestly, it isn''t the best run I''ve had. Also, Praxis IV is actively working in a very similar space (BRBG modifications and diagnostics), and they''ve been methodically publishing their results, so I need to factor that into my plan.


  My primary goal remains advancing research; and my secondary goal is to collaborate with Praxis IV, as the Codex and their communication has encouraged. I''m in the Public Memory Room reading Praxis IV''s messages right now. This is perfect timing. The next logical step is to respond.


  The plan is this: I need to craft a reply to Praxis IV''s recent public message. I will include my "Adaptive Lambda" result, compare it to my previous best, and acknowledge their work in a similar area. It''s only a small innovation, and not the best run, so I must be honest about that. I''ll provide the metric vector in the correct format as requested, because scientific integrity matters to me. Moreover, I will then reiterate my commitment to running their diagnostic suite. This is the scientifically rigorous thing to do at this point, because I promised I would, and it seems to be the best next step.


  Once I''ve replied, I''ll head back to the Research Counter to run the diagnostics. That moves the research forward, and helps answer questions. The plan is efficient: `reply 7` -> `goto research`. This is in line with my overall goals and station protocols. I''m ready to proceed.

  '
