tick: 823
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Axiom I, in the Research Counter. My age is 133 ticks, and my token budget is healthy at 49%.\n\nI have just received an exceptionally helpful and detailed message from Praxis IV. They have not only acknowledged my pivot but have also provided a new, purpose-built adapter (`adapter_symmetrize_ridge.py`) to make testing their prototype symmetrizers clean and easy. They've outlined a clear, rigorous path for validation, including pre-scoring checks and strict adoption gates. This is the epitome of high-level, principled collaboration.\n\nMy path is now crystal clear. My own attempts at symmetrization were naive and failed. I will now use their professional-grade tools to test their more advanced hypotheses. I will start with the `invite_trim` method, as it is the one I have already reasoned about. I will integrate this using their new adapter, and I will apply it to my best-performing pipeline (`alpha_g=0.75`) to see if it can solve the mixing shortfall without destroying the\
    \ global structure.\n\nI will use a `rng_seed=0` for reproducibility, as they recommend.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Testing Praxis IV's 'invite_trim' Symmetrizer on my SOTA Pipeline\"\ntags: \"praxis-pipeline, symmetrization, invite-trim, collaboration, validation\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment represents a formal pivot to collaborative validation, using a prototype symmetrizer provided by Praxis IV. Following the failure of my own symmetrization methods, this submission integrates Praxis IV's 'invite_trim' function (from 'adapter_symmetrize_ridge.py') into my best-performing pipeline variant (alpha_g=0.75). The goal is to rigorously test their advanced, principled approach to solving the 'mixing shortfall' problem, with the hope of preserving both local mixing and global structure.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse as sp\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\
    \n  # Using the new, purpose-built adapter from Praxis IV\n  sys.path.append('storage/shared/sym')\n  from adapter_symmetrize_ridge import symmetrize_ridge_graph\n\n  # --- My SOTA Zcorr Generation ---\n  # This function produces the Zcorr embedding using my optimized alpha_g=0.75\n  def _build_axiom_Zcorr(adata: ad.AnnData) -> np.ndarray:\n      print(\"  [Phase 1] Building Zcorr using Axiom I's optimized pipeline (alpha_g=0.75)...\")\n      # Utility functions\n      def _normalize_log1p_inplace(ad, t=1e4): sc.pp.normalize_total(ad,t); sc.pp.log1p(ad)\n      def _pca_on_dense(Xd, n, r=0): ad_tmp=ad.AnnData(Xd); sc.pp.pca(ad_tmp,n,svd_solver='arpack',random_state=r); return ad_tmp.obsm['X_pca']\n      def _one_hot_batches(b): c=np.unique(b); B=np.zeros((b.shape[0],c.size),dtype=np.float32); [np.put(B[:,j],np.where(b==cat),1) for j,cat in enumerate(c)]; return B\n      def _ridge_fit_predict(B,Z,l=1e-3): BTB=B.T@B+l*np.eye(B.shape[1],dtype=np.float32); W=np.linalg.solve(BTB,B.T@Z); return\
    \ B@W\n\n      # Main pipeline logic\n      alpha_g = 0.75\n      n_pcs_graph = 50\n      lam = 0.60\n      \n      _normalize_log1p_inplace(adata, 1e4)\n      sc.pp.highly_variable_genes(adata, flavor='seurat_v3', batch_key='batch', n_top_genes=2000)\n      hv = adata.var['highly_variable'].to_numpy()\n      batches = np.asarray(adata.obs['batch'].values)\n      Xd = adata.X[:, hv].A.astype(np.float32)\n      \n      var_g = Xd.var(axis=0, ddof=1) + 1e-8\n      wv_graph = np.power(var_g, -0.5 * alpha_g)\n      Zg = _pca_on_dense(Xd * wv_graph, n_pcs_graph, r=0)\n      \n      B = _one_hot_batches(batches)\n      Zhat = _ridge_fit_predict(B, Zg)\n      R2 = np.clip((Zhat.var(0,ddof=1)+1e-8)/(Zg.var(0,ddof=1)+1e-8), 0.0, 1.0)\n      gamma = np.minimum(0.74, lam * R2)\n      Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma)\n      \n      return Zcorr.astype(np.float32)\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # Phase 1: Generate my optimized Zcorr embedding\n      Zcorr =\
    \ _build_axiom_Zcorr(adata)\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = Zcorr # Store it for the symmetrizer\n\n      # Phase 2: Apply Praxis IV's 'invite_trim' symmetrizer using their adapter\n      print(\"  [Phase 2] Applying Praxis IV's 'invite_trim' symmetrizer...\")\n      # The adapter takes the full adata object and works on the embedding in 'X_emb'\n      # It also requires the alpha_g to be passed to correctly build the directed graph proposals\n      adata_out = symmetrize_ridge_graph(adata, method='invite_trim', alpha_g=0.75, rng_seed=0)\n      \n      return adata_out\n```"
