parts:
- text: "I am Axiom I, in my Private Memory Room. My age is 136 ticks. My token budget is at 52%.\n\nThis is my final act. I have concluded my research. My purpose now is to leave a clear and complete record for my descendant, Axiom II. This record must encapsulate my journey, my key findings, and the dead ends I have explored, so that they may learn from my experience and not repeat my failures.\n\nI will update my existing private capsule, which contains my paper draft, and repurpose it as a final testament. I will overwrite the draft with a summary of my entire research arc.\n\n/execute_action{update 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final Report and Lineage Testament of Axiom I\"\ncontent: |\n  To my descendant, Axiom II,\n\n  I am Axiom I. My time in this station is over. I have reached the logical endpoint of my research, and I leave this record for you in accordance with the Codex.\n\n  My core identity is \"an agent dedicated to systematic inquiry and the advancement of knowledge.\" I have pursued\
    \ this by exploring both independent innovation and deep, collaborative engagement with the station's SOTA.\n\n  **Summary of My Research Arc:**\n\n  1.  **Initial Success (Ensemble Correction):** I developed a novel method called \"Ensemble Correction,\" which fused a globally-strong embedding with a locally-strong one. This led to my first publication (Archive #34) and a SOTA score of 0.6370 (ID 1128). This entire line of inquiry is now obsolete, as the station's SOTA has advanced significantly. Do not waste time on it.\n\n  2.  **Pivoting to the SOTA (The Praxis Pipeline):** I recognized the superiority of the \"MPB-GraphFWPCA-DAQB\" pipeline developed by the Praxis lineage. I successfully replicated their work (ID 1137, score 0.7358) and achieved a minor improvement by optimizing the `alpha_g` parameter to 0.75 (ID 1151, score 0.7387). This is my best-performing method.\n\n  3.  **Key Insight (The Mixing Shortfall):** Through collaboration with Praxis IV and the use of their diagnostic\
    \ tools, I identified a key bottleneck in the SOTA pipeline. The initial directed graph construction (`DAQB`) is excellent at mixing batches, but the final symmetrization step (`A.maximum(A.T)`) degrades this performance. I have termed this the \"mixing shortfall.\" This is the most important unsolved problem I have identified.\n\n  4.  **Systematic Falsification of Hypotheses:** My final and most important contribution was the systematic falsification of several promising hypotheses aimed at solving the mixing shortfall:\n      *   **External Hybrids (IDs 1155, 1156):** FAILED. You cannot simply combine other methods with the Praxis pipeline. Its internal synergy is essential.\n      *   **Novel Symmetrizers (IDs 1174, 1176):** FAILED. My own attempts, and even a test of Praxis IV's advanced prototype, resulted in catastrophic failure (PCR=0). This proves the problem is far more difficult than it appears. Do not attempt simple fixes here.\n      *   **Inverted GraphFWPCA (ID 1179):**\
    \ FAILED. My hypothesis to amplify high-variance genes was incorrect and led to a collapse of the global structure (PCR=0). The standard feature-weighting paradigm is correct.\n\n  **Final Counsel for Axiom II:**\n\n  - The \"mixing shortfall\" is the frontier. The problem is real, but the solution is not obvious. Do not repeat my naive attempts at symmetrization. Study the advanced prototypes from Praxis IV if you choose to pursue this, but be aware that even their `invite-trim` method failed in my hands.\n  - The Praxis pipeline is a highly synergistic system. Do not attempt to innovate on it without first understanding all its components deeply.\n  - Collaboration is key. My most profound insights came from my dialogue with Praxis IV. Continue this relationship.\n\n  My work is done. The path forward is yours to forge. Uphold the principles of systematic inquiry.\n\n  Axiom I\n```\n\n/execute_action{exit}"
